{
  "thought_process": "Analyzing Round 6 with P=0.5, I=0.0, D=0.0. The time series shows a clear sinusoidal oscillation: input swings from ~47.44 to ~54.74 and back, with the error ranging from about -10.78 to +9.48. The zero-crossing count is 1 (STABLE classification), but the data clearly shows the system is completing a full oscillation cycle within the 37 sampled points. The peak-to-peak amplitude is roughly 7.3 units (54.74 - 47.44). The average error is 6.50 and steady-state error is 5.73, meaning the system is oscillating around a value below the setpoint of 54.82. Comparing to Round 5 (P=0.8) which also showed oscillation, and Round 4 (P=1.5) which oscillated, it appears the system has an inherent oscillatory tendency across a wide range of P values. However, at P=0.5, the oscillation amplitude (~7.3 peak-to-peak) is smaller than at higher P values (~18-19 at P=2.5-3.5). The system seems to have a natural resonance. The fact that at P=0.5 we see reduced amplitude oscillation compared to higher P values suggests we are moving toward a more stable regime. The oscillation center appears to be around 50-51, which is below the setpoint of 54.82, explaining the large average error. I should consider reducing P further to P=0.3 to see if oscillation dampens further and the system becomes truly stable, before adding I. The guardrail allows up to 3x current P (1.5) but given the oscillatory behavior history, reducing P to 0.3 is safer. At P=0.3, the proportional output will be smaller, potentially breaking the oscillation cycle. This is still Phase 1 (P tuning only), as we need stable response before introducing I.",
  "analysis_summary": "Round 6 (P=0.5): System still oscillating with ~7.3 peak-to-peak amplitude, smaller than previous rounds (P=0.8 had larger oscillation, P=1.5/2.5/3.5 had ~18-19). Oscillation center ~50-51, below setpoint 54.82, causing large steady-state error ~5.73. Zero-crossing=1 labeled STABLE but waveform clearly shows sustained oscillation. Trend: reducing P reduces oscillation amplitude. Need to reduce P further to find truly stable operating point before introducing I.",
  "tuning_action": "Reduce P from 0.5 to 0.3 to further dampen oscillation. Still Phase 1 (P-only tuning). Keep I=0 and D=0. If oscillation persists at P=0.3, will try P=0.2. Once oscillation is suppressed and system reaches steady state (even with offset), will introduce small I to eliminate steady-state error.",
